{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01RG9NRreMdBSDkqbxcPDHbz","type":"tool_result","content":"{\"result\":\"{\\\"answer\\\":\\\"The final consensus was that the **put** operation (WAL segment storage) could remain eventually consistent, while **get** must be strongly consistent (always see the latest put).\\\\n\\\\nSupporting facts in time order:\\\\n\\\\n- At 2026-04-21T18:15:57Z, Jun Rao asked which of the three object-store operations (put, get, list) used by Diskless could be eventually consistent, expecting that \\\\\\\"get should always see the result of the latest put.\\\\\\\" [mail:88e93198e095dbab41373878]\\\\n- Viktor Somogyi-Vass replied that, as in the tiered storage design, any coordinator operation should be strongly consistent (since it uses classic topics), and therefore the WAL segment storage layer - i.e. the put operation - could be eventually consistent because its metadata is stored in a strongly consistent manner. [mail:00dfdc315465260213bf900e][mail:3b03034816281c500f3c3b46]\\\\n- No later message in the thread reversed this, so the final consensus was that put could remain eventually consistent.\\\\n\\\\nWhat could not be determined: The finding does not explicitly state the status of the **list** operation; it only addresses put and get.\\\",\\\"error\\\":null,\\\"files\\\":[\\\"mail:88e93198e095dbab41373878\\\",\\\"mail:00dfdc315465260213bf900e\\\",\\\"mail:3b03034816281c500f3c3b46\\\"],\\\"seconds\\\":96.975,\\\"truncated\\\":false,\\\"turns\\\":8,\\\"usage\\\":{\\\"cache_read_tokens\\\":null,\\\"cache_write_tokens\\\":null,\\\"cost_usd\\\":0.00299723,\\\"input_tokens\\\":78519,\\\"output_tokens\\\":6929}}\"}"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9b90d760-2606-41c3-b729-0f0975f9cfb7","uuid":"8d8388e3-81f6-4c4e-904b-4a9fb5f8b5b1","timestamp":"2026-09-29T15:06:16.861Z","tool_use_result":{"content":"{\"result\":\"{\\\"answer\\\":\\\"The final consensus was that the **put** operation (WAL segment storage) could remain eventually consistent, while **get** must be strongly consistent (always see the latest put).\\\\n\\\\nSupporting facts in time order:\\\\n\\\\n- At 2026-04-21T18:15:57Z, Jun Rao asked which of the three object-store operations (put, get, list) used by Diskless could be eventually consistent, expecting that \\\\\\\"get should always see the result of the latest put.\\\\\\\" [mail:88e93198e095dbab41373878]\\\\n- Viktor Somogyi-Vass replied that, as in the tiered storage design, any coordinator operation should be strongly consistent (since it uses classic topics), and therefore the WAL segment storage layer - i.e. the put operation - could be eventually consistent because its metadata is stored in a strongly consistent manner. [mail:00dfdc315465260213bf900e][mail:3b03034816281c500f3c3b46]\\\\n- No later message in the thread reversed this, so the final consensus was that put could remain eventually consistent.\\\\n\\\\nWhat could not be determined: The finding does not explicitly state the status of the **list** operation; it only addresses put and get.\\\",\\\"error\\\":null,\\\"files\\\":[\\\"mail:88e93198e095dbab41373878\\\",\\\"mail:00dfdc315465260213bf900e\\\",\\\"mail:3b03034816281c500f3c3b46\\\"],\\\"seconds\\\":96.975,\\\"truncated\\\":false,\\\"turns\\\":8,\\\"usage\\\":{\\\"cache_read_tokens\\\":null,\\\"cache_write_tokens\\\":null,\\\"cost_usd\\\":0.00299723,\\\"input_tokens\\\":78519,\\\"output_tokens\\\":6929}}\"}","_meta":{"fastmcp":{"wrap_result":true}},"structuredContent":{"result":"{\"answer\":\"The final consensus was that the **put** operation (WAL segment storage) could remain eventually consistent, while **get** must be strongly consistent (always see the latest put).\\n\\nSupporting facts in time order:\\n\\n- At 2026-04-21T18:15:57Z, Jun Rao asked which of the three object-store operations (put, get, list) used by Diskless could be eventually consistent, expecting that \\\"get should always see the result of the latest put.\\\